 "title": "Why rainbows appear opposite the Sun 🌈",
 "principle": "Rainbows form when sunlight enters spherical water droplets, is refracted, internally reflected, and dispersed. The exiting light leaves each droplet at a fixed angle (~42°) relative to the incoming sun ray. Only droplets that send light toward the observer at that angle contribute, so the observer sees a circular arc centered on the point opposite the Sun. Common misconceptions: the rainbow is a material arc in the sky, and it stays in one place regardless of the viewer. In reality it is a visual effect that moves with the observer.",
 "evidence_version": 1,
 "basis": {
  "status": "model_knowledge_unverified",
  "limitations": "Simplifies droplet shape as perfect spheres, ignores variations in droplet size and atmospheric scattering; exact angle given as typical value"
 },
